'''Produce the gate evidence of the CIR back end in one go.

Run it on the machine that runs the docker images (the x86-64 host clang4),
from any directory, as a user that can run docker (directly on clang4; the old
box needed `sg docker -c "..."`):

  edg-cir-gate-evidence --tree ~/edg-cir-config/m5-int --milestone
  edg-cir-gate-evidence --tree ~/edg-cir-config/m5-int --lean writer \\
      --lean-base HEAD
  edg-cir-gate-evidence --tree ~/edg-cir-config/m5-int --milestone \\
      --check-baselines          # list the baselines the steps need

The evidence goes into a dated directory (default
<tree>/.tmp/gate-evidence/<timestamp>), one subdirectory per step with the full
log (`log.txt`) and a `result.json`. Steps whose `result.json` says PASS are
skipped when the command is repeated with --out of an earlier run (resume), and
--only/--skip select steps. At the end a PASS/FAIL table is printed and written
to `summary.md`.

Milestone gates: `--milestone` runs every step (--with-cpp and --with-m4),
so a full gate, the M5 gate in particular, always runs lazy-identity (lazy
against eager emission over both corpora and the pinned units) with the C++,
ABI, EH, optimization, attribute, chunking and support-sweep steps.

Baselines: the steps compare with baselines in ~/edg-cir-config (the defaults
of the --*-baseline options: m6r-base-asm, m6r-base-cpp-asm, m6r-base-status,
m6r-base-perf.json, m5a-base-perf.json for --perf-targets, identity-m6r,
day-base).  A missing, empty or unreadable baseline is a failure, never a
silent skip: before the first step the gate lists the baselines of the
selected steps and stops (status 2) if one is unusable; --check-baselines only
prints that list (status 1 if one is unusable) and --allow-missing-baselines
runs anyway, with the status checks off.  --record-images BASELINE writes the
IDs of the dev-env, llvm-cir and cir-env images next to a new baseline
(BASELINE.images.txt); a step that compares with a baseline made with other
images, or with no record, says so in its detail.

Lean gates: `--lean AREAS` (a comma separated list of the areas that the
change set touches) runs only the steps that those areas can affect, prints
the chosen set first and records every other step as SKIPPED with the reason
in the table; `--steps-for AREAS` only prints the set and exits.  The mapping
(AREA_STEPS; `build` is added to every area but `tooling`):

  writer     the ECIL writer, the FE side of the stream: cir-suite (the
             writer goldens), corpus, cpp-corpus, asm-diff, cpp-asm-diff,
             modes, cpp-modes, poison, opt-corpus, writer-stress,
             cpp-writer-stress, symbols, mixed-tu, support-sweep
  library    cir_src (the library): ctest (with lit), cir-suite (the cirgen
             run tests), corpus, cpp-corpus, asm-diff, cpp-asm-diff,
             opt-corpus, modes, cpp-modes, support-sweep
  fe-options CIR-gated front end options and macros: release-smoke
  linkage    symbol names, linkage, comdats: symbols, mixed-tu
  eccp       util/eccp.sh and the eccp configuration of the base (how the
             tests compile): cir-suite, corpus, cpp-corpus, opt-corpus,
             mixed-tu, support-sweep
  tests      the CIR tests and their recorded expectations: cir-suite,
             corpus, cpp-corpus
  support    the support table and its probes (cir_src/doc/support/,
             tests/tests/cir/features/; with tests or tooling): support-sweep
  tests-triage     the opt triage file: opt-corpus
  tests-crashes    the excused crashes file: crash-sweep
  tests-smoke      the smoke lists: smoke, cpp-smoke, opt-corpus,
                   release-smoke
  tests-reference  the expectations of the reference configurations
                   (edg_x86_64*, not the CIR ones): day, day32
  tooling    dev_tools only: tools-test (the self-tests of the tools)
  pylibs     dev_tools/pylibs (edgdocker and edgtest run every step): smoke,
             day, day32 (with tooling for the self-tests)
  twop       the two-process front end cpfe-cir-2p (with eccp and tooling):
             smoke, cpp-smoke, modes, cpp-modes
  goldens    edg-cir-ecil-dump, used by every writer golden (with tooling):
             cir-suite
  summary    edg-cir-summary, which buckets every edgy step (with tooling):
             smoke, cpp-smoke, cir-suite, corpus, cpp-corpus
  toolchain  a new LLVM/MLIR/ClangIR pin (the M5p gate): ctest, smoke,
             cpp-smoke, cir-suite, corpus, cpp-corpus, opt-corpus, asm-diff,
             cpp-asm-diff, mixed-tu, symbols, eh-probes, m4-stress,
             cpp-stress, crash-sweep, perf, rss, stress (deep nesting under
             256 KB: a new pin can bring recursion back), release-smoke
             (the only end-to-end run of release-only behavior, LLVM
             assertions off) and support-sweep; skipped for the reasons of
             TOOLCHAIN_SKIPS.
             A pin bump that also changes cir_src files declares
             toolchain,library (the library adds modes and cpp-modes)
  shared-src a change in a src/ file that every back end compiles (not
             CIR-gated): identity, isolation, day, day32

Seven areas are declared by the slices that need them and are not derived from
files (but the known-differences file of attr-diff maps to `attr`): `lazy`
(lazy-identity: S1, lazy emission), `speed` (perf, rss, stress, cpp-stress,
m4-stress, crash-sweep: S1 and S3 change memory, speed and recursion),
`crashes` (crash-sweep: S2), `attr` (attr-diff and the codequality report:
S2, optimization facts), `chunk` (chunk-identity, chunk-rss,
slow-below-threshold: S3, chunked lowering), `names` (names-check: the full
late-name check of the writer on the large units, for S4 checkpoints) and
`perf` (perf alone: the instruction counts against the baseline).

The cheap, relevant steps belong to the areas; the expensive ones
(crash-sweep, perf, rss, stress, cpp-stress, m4-stress) run at toolchain and
milestone gates only, so a writer or library slice that touches nothing a
crash, a memory or a speed gate would notice does not pay for them: say in
the slice's report why it cannot affect them.

The areas are checked against the change set, so that a lean gate cannot
leave out what the change touches: with --lean-base REF (the files of
`git diff --name-only --no-renames REF` and the untracked files of the tree)
or --changed-files FILE (one path a line, "-" for the standard input; for a
tree without git, like the box's), every file is mapped to one or more areas
by AREA_RULES, and --lean fails unless the steps of the declared areas
include the steps of every area that the change set needs (toolchain covers
eccp, for instance, but not shared-src).  The rules:

  cir_src/LLVM_REVISION, the Docker image files        toolchain
  cir_src/include/edg_cir/ (src/ecil_write.h includes
      those headers)                                   library, writer
  cir_src/doc/support/ (the support table, its
      renderer and checker)                            support, tooling
  other cir_src                                        library
  src/ecil_*, src/cir_be*                              writer
  src/cmd_line.*, src/macro.*                          shared-src, writer,
                                                       fe-options
  other src/ (the CIR-gated writer hooks are in
      scope_stk.c, lower_name.c, mem_manage.c,
      class_decl.c, fe_wrapup.c, and any front end
      change alters the streamed IL)                   shared-src, writer
  util/eccp.sh, bases/docker/cir-env                   eccp
  dev_tools/cir-mixed-tu-cases                         linkage
  dev_tools/pylibs                                     tooling, pylibs
  dev_tools/bin/cpfe-cir-2p                            tooling, eccp, twop
  dev_tools/bin/edg-cir-ecil-dump                      tooling, goldens
  dev_tools/bin/edg-cir-summary                        tooling, summary
  other dev_tools                                      tooling
  tests: the filter files cir-opt-triage.txt,
      cir-excused-crashes.txt and cir-*-smoke.txt         tests-triage,
                                                          -crashes, -smoke
  tests/tests/cir/features/ (the probes of the support
      table)                                           tests, support
  tests/.edgy/config.json, tests/tests/ outside cir/ (the day
      suite runs the primary suites)                     tests, tests-reference
  tests/.edgy/postures/ (the day32 posture)              tests-reference
  expectations of edg_x86_64 (the CIR configs inherit them) tests,
                                                          tests-reference
  expectations of every other configuration but edg_x86_64_cir*
                                                          tests-reference
  other tests (the CIR expectations and tests, filters)   tests
  documentation, Changes files and pending_changes/ entries  none

A file that no rule maps fails the check (add a rule).  --lean with no changed
file at all fails too (a gate with nothing to check is a mistake, usually a
wrong --lean-base) unless --allow-empty-change-set is given.  --no-area-check
skips the check (the report must say why).  A step that no listed area
selects is not run in a lean gate; say in the gate's report why the change
set cannot affect it.

Steps (names for --only/--skip):

  build      build cir_src (build/cir), cpfe-cir (build/gcc-cir) and the
             non-ASan cpfe-cir (build/gcc-release-cir) and the util tools
  identity   cpfe, cpfe-cp, cdisp and util preprocessed output and
             --dump_configuration identical to the identity-m6r capture
             (4 presets), except the preprocessed output of the files on the
             allow-list tests/.edgy/filters/cir-identity-allowed.txt
             (approved ungated changes, each with its reason; a stale entry
             fails; the list is empty now; a compare of no files fails)
  isolation  edg-cir-check-isolation
  smoke      cir-c-smoke is 100% PASS in edg_x86_64_cir and edg_x86_64_cir_2p
  cir-suite  tests/tests/cir (writer goldens, cirgen run tests) in both CIR
             configs: all pass, no difference from the recorded expectations
  corpus     the C corpus in edg_x86_64_cir: summary, CIR-specific failures
             against edg_x86_64's expectations; no CIR-specific FE_CRASH or
             LIB_CRASH (a front end crash of the same class, ABORT or
             CATASTROPHE, that edg_x86_64 records for the case is excused and
             listed as INFO in excused-crashes.txt only if its backtrace has
             no ecil_/cir_be frame or, without one, it is the same assertion
             at the same file:line and source position (crash_excuse in
             edg-cir-summary); a LIB_CRASH never is), no KILLED/TIMEOUT test,
             and
             no difference from the recorded edg_x86_64_cir expectations
             (unless --allow-status-changes), and no PASS->not PASS case
             against the statuses of --status-baseline (default the M6.R run's)
             that tests/.edgy/filters/cir-status-triage.txt does not
             list with a reason (a line for a case that did not regress is
             stale and fails); cpp-corpus has the same check
  modes      in-process and two-process output identical over the corpus
  stress     edg-cir-stress (deep nesting through the two-process path,
             each case within --stress-timeout, 3600 s)
  abi-diff   (--with-m4 or --only) edg-cir-abi-diff batch --vbases over
             dev_tools/cir-abi-cases/{s1,s2} and the clang vtable-layout and
             thunk tests (copied from --llvm-project): passes iff no block
             differs ("ours" or "fe"), none is missing except those of the
             known tool limits (template classes: clang prints the template
             name without its arguments), and every file was compared
  symbols    edg-cir-symbols over the same cases against clang, with g++
             listed: passes iff every difference is on the allowlist of
             KNOWN_SYMBOL_DIFFERENCES (with reasons) and nothing was skipped
  mixed-tu   edg-cir-mixed-tu over every case, both directions, with g++ and
             with clang as the other translation unit: passes iff there is
             exactly one row per case x direction x host (cases x 2 x 2) and
             each is PASS, or XFAIL for a direction that the case lists in
             its xfail file; an XPASS (a stale xfail line) fails
  eh-probes  edg-cir-run-diff over the writer's exception probes (probes/m4
             e*, s4_*, s5_*): programs built by g++ and by the CIR back end
             run alike; the probes of --eh-probes-skip may be diagnosed as
             unsupported; at least one probe must exist and one must pass
  opt-corpus (--with-m4 or --only) both corpora at -O2 (--cir_opt_level=2
             through EDG_CPFE_DEFAULT_OPTIONS, set for its runs only) in
             edg_x86_64_cir, and both smoke lists at -O2 in
             edg_x86_64_cir_2p.  Passes iff the corpora have the cases of the
             -O0 run (the corpus steps of this evidence directory, else
             --opt-baseline-status), every PASS->FAIL case and every new crash,
             kill or timeout has a line in
             tests/.edgy/filters/cir-opt-triage.txt (category: ub,
             dg-option, ref-fails, our-ir, upstream; the our-ir ones are
             listed in our-ir.txt), and the assembly of every smoke test
             differs between -O0 and -O2 (edg-cir-compare-modes
             --opt-level, which also checks that both modes agree at each
             level)
  m4-stress  cir_src/scripts/box_m4_stress.sh under `ulimit -s 256` over the
             cases of M4_STRESS_CASES (the sizes of research/m4_stress.md):
             passes iff every run succeeds within its time and memory limit
  tools-test the self-tests of the tools (dev_tools/bin/edg-cir-tools-test)
  support-sweep  (--with-m4 or --only; milestones) every test that the rows of
             cir_src/doc/support name (tests/tests paths) and every test of
             tests/tests/cir/features (their -O0 and -O2 cases) through edgy
             in edg_x86_64_cir, and the tests under tests/tests/cir/ also in
             edg_x86_64_cir_2p (the corpora are not run there): a result for
             every named test, every case of the e2e-run, negative and
             writer tests passes, and no line differs from the recorded
             expectations (so a failing test keeps its recorded status).  The
             lit, lib and writer paths under cir_src/test come from the ctest
             step of the same evidence (else ctest runs in build/cir) and the
             mixed-tu cases from its mixed-tu step (else the named cases run
             with g++ and clang).  Then `make_support.py --check --gate
             SUPPORT_GATE` (the constant of this file that every gate sets to
             its own id; M6.1a) must pass: the rows, the test kinds and the
             rendered files
  attr-diff  (--with-m4 or --only) edg-cir-attr-diff at -O0 and -O2 (ours
             without the LLVM passes) over the C and C++ smoke lists and the
             pinned real-world units, against the known-differences file
             tests/.edgy/filters/cir-attr-known.txt (a comment above every
             entry and a reason in it): passes iff nothing is unexplained in
             the high-value attributes (nounwind, noundef, nonnull,
             dereferenceable, align, memory, mustprogress, noreturn, builtin,
             nobuiltin, inlinehint, alwaysinline; the other differences, the
             call-set differences, norecurse, cold and the rest, are the
             residue: counted by category and listed in residue.txt, not
             gated), symbols were compared at every level, no pinned unit is
             missing
             (each compares symbols at each level), our share of definitions
             and of declarations with nounwind on the pinned units is at least
             clangir's, and no known entry is stale
  codequality  (--with-m4 or --only) edg-cir-codequality: ours against clang,
             clangir and g++ on the 10 kernels at -O2 (instructions and
             .text); a report (geometric means in the detail), gated only
             for the setup
  chunk-identity  (--with-m4 or --only) edg-cir-chunk-identity: chunked
             lowering forced to one function per chunk against unchunked at
             -O0 over both corpora and on the pinned units at -O0 and -O2:
             same exit status, diagnostics and assembly symbol by symbol, and
             non-vacuous (the pinned units lower in 2 chunks or more)
  chunk-rss  (--with-m4 or --only) edg-cir-chunk-rss: peak memory of edg-cir
             on c3000, c6000, c12000 and cxx6000 (minimum of 3 runs) by
             default, unchunked and with chunking forced on; passes iff the
             forced peak of c12000 is at most --chunk-target (0.55) of the
             unchunked peak (`--chunk-threshold=4294967295`) of the same run,
             every forced run chunked into 2 or more chunks and every
             unchunked one into none; the pinned units are reported
  names-check  (--with-m4 or --only) edg-cir-names-check: the front end
             (the checking build build/gcc-cir) on the pinned real-world units
             and generated 6000-function C and C++ files with
             ECIL_CHECK_ALL_NAMES=1, which makes the writer's late-name check
             look at every function name where it samples: no name changes,
             the exit status and the stream are those of the run without the
             variable
  slow-below-threshold  (--with-m4 or --only) edg-cir-chunk-ir: the
             instructions of edg-cir below the chunking threshold (both smoke
             lists and a generated 500-function file) with the default
             options equal those with chunking disabled
  lazy-identity  (--with-m4 or --only) edg-cir-lazy-identity: lazy against
             eager body emission (`edg-cir --eager-bodies`) at -O0 over the
             pinned real-world units and both corpora: the same exit status
             and diagnostics (emitter-only diagnostics of dropped bodies in
             the eager run are findings, listed in eager-only.txt with their
             diagnostics; they fail unless the case is in
             tests/.edgy/filters/cir-lazy-triage.txt with a reason, and
             always on a pinned unit), the same assembly symbol by symbol, the
             oracle (nothing that lazy emission dropped is defined in the
             eager assembly) and non-vacuity (deferred, replayed and dropped
             bodies above 0 on every pinned unit, read from the library's
             --stats notes: the docstring of the tool has their spelling)
  cpp-smoke  (--with-cpp or --only) cir-cpp-smoke through --cpp-configs
             (--types rp): buckets, pass rate per --c++NN mode, failures by
             C++ feature; fails on a crash, and (--cpp-smoke-all-pass) on any
             test that does not pass
  cpp-corpus the cir-cpp-corpus list (--types rp,lp), the same tables
  cpp-modes  edg-cir-compare-modes --cxx over the C++ corpus
  cpp-writer-stress  edg-cir-writer-stress --cxx
  release-smoke  the C smoke and C++ smoke lists through the release cpfe-cir
             (--posture cir-release, build/gcc-release-cir, the shipping macro
             configuration), both configs: the C list must pass completely,
             no C++ test may pass in the debug front end and not in the
             release one, no crash
  poison     edg-cir-poison-check: region-lifetime safety.  The debug front
             end (freed IL blocks overwritten with 0xdb, expensive checking),
             the same without the overwriting (build/gcc-cir-nopoison, made
             by the step) and the release front end must show no crash or
             assertion and write byte-identical streams (poison vs. none) for
             the C corpus, the C and C++ smoke lists, tests/tests/cir
             (writer and cirgen) and the C++ corpus; the release front end
             must write the same streams as the debug one (the build-type
             dependent macros are pinned in default/cpfe-cir.cmakedef)
  cpp-stress  cir_src/scripts/box_stress.sh over the synthetic C++ streams
             (cxx_locals ... cxx_funcs) through edg-cir under a 256 KiB
             stack: every case rc=0 and run=0 (the streams are self-checking
             programs), within --cpp-stress-seconds (30) and
             --cpp-stress-rss-mb (400)
  cpp-stress-asan  the same with the ASan library build (build/cir-asan,
             built by the step), smaller sizes and --cpp-stress-asan-rss-mb
             (1200)
  writer-stress  edg-cir-writer-stress: the ASan debug front end with
             ECIL_GID_STRESS=1 over the C smoke list and tests/tests/cir/
             writer: same exit status and byte-identical streams as without
             the variable, no sanitizer report or assertion, ecil-dump clean
  rss        peak memory and time of the front end and the library on a
             generated C file, a generated C++ file and a template-heavy
             C++ file, with ASan (quarantine off) and without: every row has
             a ceiling (RSS_CEILINGS_MB) that it may not exceed
  day        the default full day suite (edg_x86_64, edg_x86_64_cp) has the same
             test statuses as the M0 baseline run
  day32      the same for the 32-bit configurations (edg_x86_32,
             edg_x86_32_cp: the i686 build, posture file
             tests/.edgy/postures/day32.json, --day32-posture) against
             day-base/baseline-32 (--day32-baseline-run)
  ctest      the cir_src tests in every cir_src preset, each built from
             scratch (its build directory is wiped first, unless
             --keep-ctest-builds): ninja decides what to rebuild by file
             times, and a tree synced with `rsync -a` keeps the sources'
             old times, so an older build directory would not be rebuilt
  asm-diff   the in-process assembly of every corpus test case of this tree
             against that of an earlier commit (--asm-baseline, a directory
             that `edg-cir-compare-modes --save-asm D --in-process-only`
             wrote, e.g. at the previous gate): identical files, files that
             differ only in the order of their symbols, only in the numbering
             of the labels inside a function, in the symbols they define,
             or in a function's code, and cases whose exit status or
             assembly existence changed; a source hint (for-init, alloca)
             is printed with every difference.  Passes
             only if all are identical (or, with --asm-allow-order, only
             reordered); report.txt has the details
  cpp-asm-diff  asm-diff for the C++ corpus (--cpp-corpus-filter, compiled as
             C++) against --cpp-asm-baseline, made in an old tree with
             `edg-cir-compare-modes --cxx
             @tests/.edgy/filters/cir-cpp-corpus.txt --types rp,lp -j 12
             --save-asm D --in-process-only`
  crash-sweep  every test type of the C corpus, of
             the C++ corpus (its filters) and of imported/gnu/cpp/eh/, rtti/
             and cir/ through edg_x86_64_cir, run by run (--sweep-timeout per
             test): every FE_CRASH, LIB_CRASH, kill and timeout that
             edg_x86_64 does not have too (the same crash_excuse rules) fails
             the step and is listed with a reproducer command in
             crashes.txt; the statuses of the tests themselves (fp, fn, ...
             have no CIR expectations) are not gated
  perf       edg-cir-perf (instruction counts, CPU time and RSS of a big
             generated file and of the whole corpus) and, with --perf-baseline
             (the JSON of an earlier run, default the M6.R run's; a step
             without one fails unless --perf-measure-only), the before/after
             table;
             --perf-max-regression PERCENT (default
             DEFAULT_PERF_MAX_REGRESSION) makes an increase of an
             instruction count beyond it a failure; a baseline row that the
             new run lacks (or has as 0) fails too.  --perf-realworld pinned
             adds the pinned real-world units (their library and front end
             instructions join the rows above; --boost-include for Boost)
             and --perf-targets s1 the S1 targets (library instructions of
             every pinned unit at most 70%, of the generated C++ unit at -O0
             at most 92% of --perf-targets-baseline, the M5a run:
             edg-cir-perf --targets)

For the step asm-diff, the baseline of a commit is made with this tree's
scripts on a tree of that commit (a `git archive` of it will do):

  edg-exec --img cir-env -- python3 <this tree>/dev_tools/bin/edg-cir-compare-modes \\
      @tests/.edgy/filters/cir-c-corpus.txt --types rp,lp -j 12 \\
      --save-asm .tmp/asm --in-process-only      (in the old tree, built)

Use --smoke-filter, --corpus-filter and --types to test the script on a small
selection first.

Syncing a tree to the box: use `rsync -a --checksum` (or wipe build/cir*,
build/gcc-cir and build/gcc-release-cir) when the tree has been built before.
`rsync -a` keeps the modification times of the sources, which can be older than
the objects of an earlier build of the same directory from a previous sync, so
ninja then rebuilds nothing and the evidence is for the old code.
'''

import argparse
import difflib
import fnmatch
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import shutil
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import tarfile
import time

from datetime import datetime
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Callable, Dict, List, Optional, Tuple

STEPS = ('build', 'identity', 'isolation', 'smoke', 'cir-suite', 'corpus',
         'modes',
         'stress', 'writer-stress', 'rss', 'day', 'day32', 'ctest', 'asm-diff',
         'perf',
         'cpp-smoke', 'cpp-corpus', 'cpp-modes', 'cpp-writer-stress',
         'cpp-stress', 'cpp-stress-asan', 'poison', 'release-smoke',
         'cpp-asm-diff', 'crash-sweep', 'abi-diff', 'symbols', 'mixed-tu',
         'eh-probes', 'm4-stress', 'opt-corpus', 'tools-test',
         'lazy-identity', 'attr-diff', 'codequality', 'chunk-identity',
         'chunk-rss', 'slow-below-threshold', 'names-check',
         'support-sweep')
# The steps of gate M4 (the ABI and EH harnesses); they run only with
# --with-m4 or --only.
M4_STEPS = ('abi-diff', 'symbols', 'mixed-tu', 'eh-probes', 'm4-stress',
            'opt-corpus', 'lazy-identity', 'attr-diff', 'codequality',
            'chunk-identity', 'chunk-rss', 'slow-below-threshold',
            'names-check', 'support-sweep')
# The steps of the C++ milestones; they run only with --with-cpp or --only.
CPP_STEPS = ('cpp-smoke', 'cpp-corpus', 'cpp-modes', 'cpp-writer-stress',
             'cpp-stress', 'cpp-stress-asan', 'poison', 'release-smoke',
             'cpp-asm-diff')
# Steps that take long and run only with --only (none at present).
LONG_STEPS: Tuple[str, ...] = ()
# The steps of a lean gate (--lean, --steps-for) by the area of the change.
AREA_STEPS = {
  'writer': ('cir-suite', 'corpus', 'cpp-corpus', 'asm-diff',
             'cpp-asm-diff', 'modes', 'cpp-modes', 'poison', 'opt-corpus',
